Move every operating system call of termbox2 behind a platform layer

termbox2 talks to the operating system in a dozen places: it opens the
terminal, puts it into raw mode and back, asks for its size, waits for
input with select(2) and reads it, writes its output, catches SIGWINCH
through a pipe, reads the clock and spells out errors with strerror_r.
This patch gathers the calls of tb_init_file, tb_init_rwfd, tb_deinit,
wait_event, tb_set_input_mode, bytebuf_flush and tb_strerror into static
functions at the end of the implementation, with one POSIX definition
each:

  open_tty, init_fds, restore_term_attrs, deinit_resize_handler,
  close_opened_fds, enable_mouse_input, wait_input, write_output,
  get_term_name, now_ms, format_last_error

init_term_attrs, init_resize_handler and update_term_size, which already
are functions of their own, stay where they are; the next patch gives
them Windows definitions in place. The callers use the functions above
instead of the system. The behaviour on POSIX systems does not change;
the tb_init_file path now goes through tb_init_rwfd like every other,
and tb_deinit closes both descriptors tb_init_file opened when they
differ. The comments on
tb_init and tb_get_fds already describe the Windows backend that the
next patch adds on top of this layer.

Term::Fabulous builds its own additions (src/termbox2.c) on these
functions, so that they share termbox2's platform code.
